u}(Z_{n})}]$, where $Z_{n}=(e^{i	heta_{1}},\ldots,e^{i	heta_{n}})$ and $p_{\mu}$ is the power-sum symmetric function for partition $\mu$. When $ eta=2$, our inequalities recover an identity by Diaconis and Evans for Haar-invariant unitary matrices. Further, we have the following: $\lim_{n	o\infty}\mathbb{E}[p_{\mu}(Z_{n})\overline{p_{
u}(Z_{n})}]=\delta_{\mu
u}( rac{2}{ eta})^{l(\mu)}z_{\mu}$ for any $ eta>0$ and partitions $\mu,
u$; $\lim_{m	o\infty}\mathbb{E}[|p_{m}(Z_{n})|^{2}]=n$ for any $ eta>0$ and $n\geq2$, where $l(\mu)$ is the length of $\mu$ and $z_{\mu}$ is explicit on $\mu$. These results apply to the three important ensembles: COE ($ eta=1$), CUE ($ eta=2$) and CSE ($ eta=4$). We further examine the nonasymptotic behavior of $\mathbb{E}[|p_{m}(Z_{n})|^{2}]$ for $ eta=1,4$. The central limit theorems of $\sum_{j=1}^{n}g(e^{i	heta_{j}})$ are obtained when (i) $g(z)$ is a polynomial and $ eta>0$ is arbitrary, or (ii) $g(z)$ has a Fourier expansion and $ eta=1,4$. The main tool is the Jack function.

We condition super-Brownian motion on “boundary statistics” of the exit measure $X_{D}$ from a bounded domain $D$. These are random variables defined on an auxiliary probability space generated by sampling from the exit measure $X_{D}$. Two particular examples are: conditioning on a Poisson random measure with intensity $ eta X_{D}$ and conditioning on $X_{D}$ itself. We find the conditional laws as $h$-transforms of the original SBM law using Dynkin’s formulation of $X$-harmonic functions. We give explicit expression for the (extended) $X$-harmonic functions considered. We also obtain explicit constructions of these conditional laws in terms of branching particle systems. For example, we give a fragmentation system description of the law of SBM conditioned on $X_{D}=
u$, in terms of a particle system, called the backbone. Each particle in the backbone is labeled by a measure $	ilde{
u}$, representing its descendants’ total contribution to the exit measure. The particle’s spatial motion is an $h$-transform of Brownian motion, where $h$ depends on $	ilde{
u}$. At the particle’s death two new particles are born, and $	ilde{
u}$ is passed to the newborns by fragmentation.
